#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179571 :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b638f71-529a-5a6f-a68e-805f11080587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645 :topic/math-academy-id 2635 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Paths Expressed as Functions of X"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769 :topic/math-academy-id 875 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Parabolas"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9471e9d-8fd8-54f6-b2a2-21979387ec44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177418} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaf38e8-dea0-53ca-af4c-7595b6aa19d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177419} {:answer/id #uuid "da81cbb7-1c0d-5fb6-a136-436cce0614d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177420} {:answer/id #uuid "1a417b80-1c7d-54df-a4b9-d83760ab35a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177421} {:answer/id #uuid "548acf66-02a4-5f9b-bf62-28fc428f02f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177422}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7770a3af-79d6-5207-9481-e0e095e0784b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177417}] :question/id #uuid "4163da76-7dc5-52f8-8a6d-5dcabcafe172" :question/math-academy-id "q-122139" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfcc5e2-5fae-5e85-98a0-4b4a8389615e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177484} {:answer/id #uuid "b60f042f-515e-55c8-9669-504d7122f51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177485} {:answer/id #uuid "fa827bf5-62aa-53a4-8c0b-0bb5bf842b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177486} {:answer/id #uuid "275b43e3-32aa-59d3-b6f6-d5224224ccce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6793c3-401c-517a-8cdb-2cd95d932197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6218cae7-f715-5e6e-b9dc-dfe12bb38c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177483}] :question/id #uuid "8b50d48f-8fc4-5b58-ae9a-175e59cd59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122446" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7542f2f6-b892-5626-afb8-db34cd70e0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254709} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ea6601-5749-54c9-ba36-44e09b3b0dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255681} {:answer/id #uuid "e7df80cd-42a0-564e-887c-f1473180be67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255709} {:answer/id #uuid "f5478718-e38f-51df-ba4d-0a7c66983c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6ea31-331c-56c0-bd88-8b436b2b7eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffffbcf7-2290-5771-97a7-2e3fca38c6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256369}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37df0ad-2178-59ae-82b8-7b50bbb1ab58" :question/math-academy-id "q-122530" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{3})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9{(\\frac{t}{3})}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9})(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba4acff-ed4d-5004-82bf-b89074e988ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254189} {:answer/id #uuid "76b7eee7-6a99-5701-a4bd-ca2f9ad5ab1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{2e}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254718} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd7ab28-aada-5ff7-ae14-4e7c1b105102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255041} {:answer/id #uuid "f299ef39-a3f8-5921-9d4d-66255a05f5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}t\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0041b6-b7a6-544b-a5a1-4601e83eb423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253911} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b25ddd-1b34-5ba6-b53a-dc771bfba0c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256201}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2f457a6-0776-5f23-8cdb-bf0ad17c2cda" :question/math-academy-id "q-122534" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b799c33-4069-5d75-9acf-2c1fb425b3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272775} {:answer/id #uuid "5eec3c9c-1f5e-50d8-ad72-41bdf8012479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273202} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45bf0f-9d2b-5f0b-8d67-58b93e277d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273578} {:answer/id #uuid "9885a09f-ec2b-514c-a625-30bef7ad1a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0269b0-7a87-54a9-bbbd-f720ed0c881a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba25d72c-66e5-5660-8cd6-858347d6e9a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-149714" :question/problem "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c793c767-8ecf-564e-af97-7c0e5166064b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387217} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2e31f-1ce7-5e4f-bb9e-8d3e5faf7e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387218} {:answer/id #uuid "b13d0e88-37bf-5316-8237-40c1c7c1a505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387219} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a101a7-ad66-5ad3-8847-9c8591f60fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+8}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae35c1a8-9ade-537f-b9ff-8d9b8d021811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387220} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4412e87-ff23-50d1-8bf7-efc4e936bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bf8f8b4-ea1a-5394-a193-4b55a8b9d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122542" :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4(\\frac{2}{t})(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca1dcb5-771f-59eb-81e9-1e595c34e462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398231} {:answer/id #uuid "676387be-6610-5f16-9796-2babd5ce0f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398232} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbb623b-497e-5583-a206-85cecb99534f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398233} {:answer/id #uuid "a43b3ff9-d167-5f2a-a49d-342a3fc83921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d025741-a121-5bf5-a673-350aefe4b1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398229}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "757cf839-4729-500e-9a5f-1ad15eb1165b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122527" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/f9/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3d/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/99/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049882 :topic/math-academy-id 1900 :topic/title "Level Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027 :topic/math-academy-id 1939 :topic/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c2aec3-ab10-5812-a6f0-e32442afa1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c01770dd5222fc43a65dd92381917821c127d39a0055ca464582c829ef8d3fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177473} {:answer/id #uuid "cad68cfa-0e83-5847-b359-8bce8928018f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4fa5e55308b46106812c7a3c2620b528487b520ea282144cc6baa2fd3cb345b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177474} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8d52-9d50-500b-9881-dac55517541e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177475} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec2d219-5a14-5c00-a4d6-e94fc02507d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd1fc14-019a-5f80-944a-ad3518a49e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a11e4f-11c8-502a-b54b-73b03e51f3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-122413" :question/problem "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175c18fc-c927-5f10-aba9-f8c07cd1f45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/229bb0ca171a2f77f16e15471bce4037e642299e7141025bfb178a81f901ee02.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253871} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e5a9-3b6b-5a1d-98c3-3f8c03d6c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} {:answer/id #uuid "81f53b31-d962-5549-8632-88d0cfde19be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d55feb791b51d0b4ebfe6b8998f1aad1e1a8d57d1593097b0c99d706a01d47a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254822} {:answer/id #uuid "c16229c8-fca2-5a01-8eeb-0140284c0d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/372482df951dbb50d6db837939a0708a9a69ea330c03e59bce19398575ad50f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255395} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc85735-898d-52d1-b585-43d59e88f534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c3f79f33b0a983f7c92acebaddbd3bc5208275cca585ac5464ddf44c9a7e8d87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e5a9-3b6b-5a1d-98c3-3f8c03d6c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac1c634-c613-5135-9d7d-f12696fc2a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256028}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f7bc31-3a53-5257-80a4-a0a85f0ea7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122572" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb80c1c-95cb-5565-a077-e01dae2538e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254362} {:answer/id #uuid "5575b773-fb45-568e-9e09-8e292ff7268e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6598c50e72417a0bff5514fa2b8ff9fba7c617cff10dc83e15472259d040846.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254415} {:answer/id #uuid "9d45adbb-a2ba-5573-a768-bab8773e9e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255052} {:answer/id #uuid "bb73aff5-0f60-5f0e-a385-48945789fb36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255341} {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66620e0-e12e-5bd1-b12e-36f3f0afecb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255826} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d74b99d-539a-5a25-adea-708b48ca371b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef1fc6b0-b283-5ecf-84c4-88b300df87f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122796" :question/problem "![](images/20/201804549f48637076fcaad5252632527b937e01cc75c153bc1db26b246d2aa6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/f4/f4dc7eaf7ed7651ee38157c486f185ef3eecaf9ccb7e91df18e897c56c5c5e23.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/9c/9ce2dd3e0c639e4f520556c7f4955451eb599b35e0714aa326cb6efd74cfaa5c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} {:answer/id #uuid "03aaca50-6ba1-5767-8a1d-61289921be5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c1a0035b37bc0137dc2ac164c980d538f9362feb3b439e663885f8aa7178124.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253699} {:answer/id #uuid "5936829d-c5a5-5317-93a8-8d046c07d07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa8bf282264f1647644cc15b6aece745718bd17617afad2c2530292bfed10b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254452} {:answer/id #uuid "70f6299c-8313-5e6f-a9c7-80abdfba5fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/704a1e0712c0c2512e16ac03f1eeb7ecd55996c822eced54ac510f4719485c42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254671} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf9db65-02b1-5896-a31b-6345273456e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f9d6304bc9cc6b6e74235c66a7d70b96dbea2db44e9ed82606f6025075b76336.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00678095-4785-5a48-bfd2-04906406b23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46a2d28-3dee-5856-b9b2-7ac4fb2b706b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f67d0b79-608f-51fc-afe3-9623997cda39" :question/math-academy-id "q-122810" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/77/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4e/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16f010-8927-5a11-821b-dadc53e436d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d5e0a4e1a1784167984a6dde093b97b14ebb0910ce56cd1d879782bfad6b7e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253779} {:answer/id #uuid "118abcd0-7890-5af2-b48d-3e8745d6c268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3fc34a2294e81abf04bfacf7d4367c0492a173af45f8f453a86b95a3a6028d4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253822} {:answer/id #uuid "6523a047-fedb-5a31-a148-d33413134901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6b8dc1bcaf76374a580505308a9282b65e5da1b7d0961e2a6c30ba8ce6b2dd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254577} {:answer/id #uuid "716fad33-4080-5fc3-bc31-939e1cf8b681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cb/cb66a357917b9683a61b04aeab03ff60e359160f3c5e51dc4b282d6464666467.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254676} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ff449c6-cc37-5b79-8ef9-32639dce72bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254934} :answer-field/id #uuid "e493ba4b-c644-55d9-8213-f4543a4426f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256327}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7e9bf88-89a8-589d-8711-7c4a07b86d32" :question/math-academy-id "q-122829" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=4,$ which gives an ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/83/835b4206e04d064485d02635cfd4e2ad39a934b1d2b38296cc6463a265f5d931.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8e0a10d162d30be895fa4ce49e3944e4e1a3ee9f79aa2fafe70c0edd2c0d61.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5fd011917170ef1f098b875060ad48d3ea5d33a1d16d369ae58614c53cd29c9c.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f45f2-3480-501a-b813-16b2c86698bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253685} {:answer/id #uuid "27b268f0-4ffc-54a5-9920-488afedf2f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254023} {:answer/id #uuid "67bd03df-6493-53ce-b107-795c206a4430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e76b318c2f347bc59746ea0dc88c838ad46694724563867a0ee94318c826d887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254608} {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} {:answer/id #uuid "9e64a607-79af-580d-9eca-019fa0b4a565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88069253-0215-50cc-b496-a51baed841a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254878} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e428f18-510c-5a07-b305-8e470386def5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43fea3a1-3b04-5702-a0ac-bdd3d7956101" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/03/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/34/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6926dfa-4ca0-5d9b-bb91-d663dc9fd39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9b8e151985327da40d1fb3044c7f4c785a9f0fcbaa5d71d7f865d4b3fee38ab9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383022} {:answer/id #uuid "2226c493-e827-540e-b916-5dff3335baf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383023} {:answer/id #uuid "1faef452-1968-5f42-b62d-394dc63eb8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} {:answer/id #uuid "77afea4d-6f7d-5b97-a1b1-fcdcc7652a4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04f52ccb3eed1f0377edebf34607812f2c8eefe27e910e250959e8fa39ef13d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383025} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f42144-a862-5e50-bcf2-5c21c8fe56a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae9e6dc29361d66784dae2868e500940132049cfa04b65aafddc06d35c1afdbf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1faef452-1968-5f42-b62d-394dc63eb8f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383024} :answer-field/id #uuid "800aaf33-50f4-54f1-a1a6-6a6d09e8c947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8afa4