s in the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f22df07aac0933106381dce4571e674d656428bf7a976e16bed6d46898f814f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758d7c9c-7190-58ba-88ca-6ba4209fb9ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02e2b36b593aef6b8477a6e3a546a7a367cd6c7c8de4642684f418222e0d1c96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407560} {:answer/id #uuid "b0547ab6-4eb4-5605-bedc-e8c09b74d5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f79cfa6b73725479bd76db48b212a60b04ed91f3355c9d135e8cf176b3a6b8f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407561} {:answer/id #uuid "b6af969e-293e-5aa6-8efd-61f10b87dbac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/426f761b56bbba7549a5d76ecc6340461c3bc76627c9664a46ef4c6063ee7039.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407562} {:answer/id #uuid "e719fb82-e005-592a-9a1d-c08ce892673d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5f4468465de36289d11b86acb35c38e6d2034ca220d6559ec93b5085d7437d5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407563} {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-122880" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Relationship Between the Vector Field of a Function and Its Contour Plot"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd5c5fd8-409c-5b92-a0f1-4466c76cdbe0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143 :topic/math-academy-id 1934 :topic/title "The Gradient Vector"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b255ed32-00b2-5c02-8fdf-4137e45f1f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3y - x - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177454} {:answer/id #uuid "07aeabaa-1756-5d7c-b175-364dae9da70e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x - y)i + (6y - x - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177455} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac9617b-4aa8-5c9d-9329-1e2364c1eed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3x - y - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177456} {:answer/id #uuid "56d77004-8bf3-5e3b-9345-4eb6281965f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2y - x)i + (6y - y - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177457} {:answer/id #uuid "5732c3e7-79ac-51b8-b415-739696a1c978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6y - x - 4)i + (2x - y)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177458}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca579634-59cd-5d79-9ef4-86d15c0bb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177453}] :question/id #uuid "9fe868ae-14fa-5f36-b879-4b4d02b56eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-122399" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - xy + 3y^{2} - 4y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba475e2-be08-57e4-adec-8abff6266246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} {:answer/id #uuid "1b833134-f253-5096-a14a-d79dffc93bf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253903} {:answer/id #uuid "1d588fd7-e3d7-55a7-9d28-9f3428789f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253917} {:answer/id #uuid "69fb1b09-9263-5539-88e5-0d8613d52b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254624} {:answer/id #uuid "6e94b4d4-2cb7-572a-9f0d-7088c7794500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba475e2-be08-57e4-adec-8abff6266246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} :answer-field/id #uuid "421569dd-cfb3-59c7-8de4-b002df6d520d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256046}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68db203b-e688-5d3a-9ec7-9ce2f2bf5402" :question/math-academy-id "q-122404" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0820aec6-52e0-5e49-a59a-3bbb2eaf3087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253728} {:answer/id #uuid "28546720-1acd-59ba-82be-bb4ffaf85ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+xz\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254032} {:answer/id #uuid "707b941f-3995-54da-8e24-b82c909648d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254666} {:answer/id #uuid "86a81cb8-c83d-53e2-85a7-df445fd4a517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254861} {:answer/id #uuid "d766fd6b-8bc8-553e-b31b-478e1a2b8eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,j+2z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "707b941f-3995-54da-8e24-b82c909648d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254666} :answer-field/id #uuid "96de8d74-918e-533e-ac2c-2823c4173a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec9f81d0-f152-5c1c-b7c8-fd08eb67e7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122596" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6a5e9d-527e-52ec-ba7f-90eb833346f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254187} {:answer/id #uuid "4210211b-29fa-55e2-a0b7-52ff66e974ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254233} {:answer/id #uuid "445bfc7e-e291-543e-855e-38c46c84f040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254250} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e3b28e-4aac-520e-a4d4-79a1adb3c2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255541} {:answer/id #uuid "f337cd0f-4b55-5597-858a-91b7156059ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f337cd0f-4b55-5597-858a-91b7156059ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255798} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0aba96-bf2c-522a-b26d-f17993fb8c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255931}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcca6806-23f0-5850-a949-44a533788116" :question/math-academy-id "q-122607" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2x-1}-\\sqrt{4y+1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}\\,i-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83468821-c39c-5688-9232-e0a48bb155ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382980} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b76065-e59a-5ca7-9bfb-b669207ba5b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x+4y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2x+4y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382981} {:answer/id #uuid "e411cee5-3a6c-5dda-a344-184fd78d28cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{2x+4y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{2x+4y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382982} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cc5482-1799-5098-a009-2ed159b19110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x+y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382983} {:answer/id #uuid "62c2233e-e9a0-53da-b0de-07b7e76c7fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83468821-c39c-5688-9232-e0a48bb155ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b277d7-41a9-51d5-9221-157f88442b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdc51975-a7a3-5414-a1fb-3bb1994c28af" :question/math-academy-id "q-122599" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x+2y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc65a4b-da4d-5350-953b-f73c08bd26cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+(x+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382987} {:answer/id #uuid "36b97e42-68ff-502f-862c-54abe712f096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382988} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f60994-da11-59b0-bb31-edf3cb7c1617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382989} {:answer/id #uuid "344a1d8c-370f-5d8f-9dd1-49c0513501e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382990} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2fc14b-bb86-5f37-bc2d-be43a95e0d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b97e42-68ff-502f-862c-54abe712f096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382988} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3294c69-216b-5d6f-aa53-6f52d4c57c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382986}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde4da3d-ec55-5f32-ac16-c33bd9acfbfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122600" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y) \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz+2y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz+2y)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1393202f-d724-535d-8136-babf2686d7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{xyz+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{xyz+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{xyz+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382994} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f87687-52a9-5e53-b8e0-28200e4a81f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xyz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382995} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb0e8f2-8c32-5a43-9f8c-3b04ae890241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{z}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382996} {:answer/id #uuid "b14f2ad2-72a2-5e49-bfaf-d442c2b9f197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382997} {:answer/id #uuid "026ff4bf-c0ad-56d4-bab2-b4e07f1395b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b14f2ad2-72a2-5e49-bfaf-d442c2b9f197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382997} :answer-field/id #uuid "640d3877-8db5-5d72-a910-f0495ca7df56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382993}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c11364f-7b1f-5610-8765-613e6f80f65c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122601" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605c17ba-53c0-5224-8301-c7161947f7df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(6x{y}^{2}+{z}^{4})\\,j+(x-4y{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383001} {:answer/id #uuid "100cf2d7-b1cf-56bd-b4c5-b50e52dddf3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383002} {:answer/id #uuid "cb18d096-f5eb-5578-ad2c-c68d07d3455d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383003} {:answer/id #uuid "8aee926a-f13b-525d-8f4f-67de6743fd6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{2})\\,i+(6xy-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-8{y}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383004} {:answer/id #uuid "5fca2b8f-91e9-5173-9378-858ea3cdc4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+{y}^{2})\\,i+(3xy-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-8y{z}^{2})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb18d096-f5eb-5578-ad2c-c68d07d3455d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383003} :answer-field/id #uuid "732edbd7-fdd5-57cd-93ab-a2bf9f45302a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383000}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9bc952c-6483-5cda-bd93-71113b0eb352" :question/math-academy-id "q-122617" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2xz+{y}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd8e3fe-9258-5fd4-ac38-46ac64db6bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387258} {:answer/id #uuid "da8671ca-6c00-5ad9-a9e3-7c2197ed1890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2y{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387259} {:answer/id #uuid "e5372737-b3d4-5fdf-b4fd-fc53a2121ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387260} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcb5dce-0d72-5149-b95d-b553c17f8c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387261} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e00fb2-b549-5885-b25c-39af3314dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e00fb2-b549-5885-b25c-39af3314dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387262} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c2ee55-5d94-5079-b34c-8536d3fd9a20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9b6323d-ff08-50ce-be1a-fa1f61c66864" :question/math-academy-id "q-122608" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,i-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Vector Field of a Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28010cfc-ecec-5ddb-8288-a2ad58658e23" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814fc6cf-8415-51dc-aa71-2b2dcbf81962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254814} {:answer/id #uuid "86f05304-f4c1-54b6-a4c5-000eafd2bbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254865} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6b72a-eb09-5d1b-85c0-269d51d0e83c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255324} {:answer/id #uuid "be619077-ef60-58f6-93be-483e6d9cbf83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255369} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ce09ca-94dc-54bf-a873-caeb486ff0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256080}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{t}{4})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot (\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8})}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967cb4cb-b2bc-5b09-a044-cead97fab3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177502} {:answer/id #uuid "124e4ecc-80db-5945-b2a0-0a70bacf7766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177503} {:answer/id #uuid "35a85fe9-c0c0-5c48-acbe-861e57fb6043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177504} {:answer/id #uuid "637fe0b8-5f7e-55d6-8cf4-e9808987ffcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{213}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177505} {:answer/id #uuid "707fa4c1-45e1-573b-8f56-1b0a414f3bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{149}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177506}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0597155-ae64-5f5d-9a35-8dbfb090d8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177501}] :question/id #uuid "a4f83414-82d1-57f1-a541-5f71790a0a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122579" :question/problem "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds$,\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47302108-d687-5b9f-8466-13bda0aa9a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254273} {:answer/id #uuid "635dfe49-19a8-5b28-a7d6-0119af1c3aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254563} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7f9994-8ead-51fc-a0bc-e35e1c013a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254628} {:answer/id #uuid "843a82b2-282f-5e4f-91da-3c42e53d7862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6(36-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254846} {:answer/id #uuid "8acd42aa-8188-53b4-8b19-2defe96db9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8acd42aa-8188-53b4-8b19-2defe96db9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(25-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254894} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0aed9aa-ec0d-5834-81f4-f0a6213310d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9c9eb7b-4691-5649-a35d-df87aec5b233" :question/math-academy-id "q-122574" :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244cbd35-4706-5f3b-a48a-e1061c8c3156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253996} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad97c8-3c56-5c9a-a8c2-77eadd3dfd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254080} {:answer/id #uuid "378b4c07-594e-5889-86f8-ae72d53a2f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254151} {:answer/id #uuid "941292e1-b3c5-55ef-a3f3-007f8b0b0228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254972} {:answer/id #uuid "f98e4257-7cc8-5955-bed8-55177dfe7d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98e4257-7cc8-5955-bed8-55177dfe7d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255855} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3046664-9e6f-53e3-9c0f-658dd83eef2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256215}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7198c6d2-e4a6-5178-afaa-7f9ff2209737" :question/math-academy-id "q-122593" :question/problem "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b6c179-73bb-5998-9a66-e7ee5c7354ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272594} {:answer/id #uuid "31820ed3-8684-5823-b207-558c302546c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272830} {:answer/id #uuid "48925fc8-d494-54fd-9553-6cb85067e122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} {:answer/id #uuid "604802b6-34cb-5971-9a07-92a58988eaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273218} {:answer/id #uuid "70fe7ed9-a4bf-5385-8fdc-2d4b842e7f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48925fc8-d494-54fd-9553-6cb85067e122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e949706-b44d-5eac-8496-453a3fc3a2e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9463919f-5148-546d-ad7d-95a446b32afe" :question/math-academy-id "q-149727" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c557a-d0d0-5ebb-811c-2baf6a3cefd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272593} {:answer/id #uuid "781b70bc-38cd-5810-ad6e-167d9400e156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "d73b4363-3c1d-563d-8b69-1e8275c1c9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3eabed-9398-5497-8cc2-1d7f384966fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274495} {:answer/id #uuid "f863851f-99c9-59c9-9adb-eb61d4881fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d73b4363-3c1d-563d-8b69-1e8275c1c9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} :answer-field/id #uuid "c355ca0d-7553-591c-b07c-62d0f6a0048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b09217dd-ddfe-59be-95cc-af28d1d6d361" :question/math-academy-id "q-149745" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}]}
